                <h2>This Week's Observation: Appeals Queue Is Longer Than Usual</h2>
<p>As we approach late Q3, multiple overseas marketing teams have reported anomalies with their Google Ads accounts—some have had campaigns paused, others entire MCC accounts suspended. A product manager at a utility app shared that from submitting their appeal to receiving a system response, it took a full 7 business days, with the system citing only "policy violation" without specifics.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. This week, appeals-related inquiries to our support channel increased approximately 35% week-over-week, concentrated on two main issues: <strong>unclear violation reasons</strong> and <strong>uncertainty about how to write appeal materials</strong>.</p>
<p>Account suspension is stressful enough, but poorly written appeals and repeated rejections can drive teams to frustration. Let's break down the essentials of preparing a winning appeal.</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. First Things First: Identify the Real Reason for Suspension</h2>
<p>The quality of your appeal depends heavily on accurately identifying the violation. Many teams jump straight to writing "We promise to comply with policies going forward" without even mentioning which policy was violated—the system will likely reject this as an invalid appeal.</p>
<p><strong>Top 3 Violation Types This Week:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Landing Page Issues</strong>: Page inaccessibility, redirect anomalies, or content misaligned with ads</li>
<li><strong>Creative Violations</strong>: Misleading claims or unauthorized third-party brand elements</li>
<li><strong>Account Structure Anomalies</strong>: Rapid creation of new campaigns or frequent budget adjustments in a short period</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Recommended Action:</strong> Log into the Google Ads Policy Center and find the specific policy related to your industry. If your notification email includes a violation ID, search for it directly in the Policy Center for detailed explanations.</p>
<hr />
<h2>2. Appeal Materials Checklist</h2>
<p>Once you've confirmed the violation reason, you can start preparing your appeal materials. This checklist has been validated across multiple successful cases:</p>
<h3>Basic Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>Google Account ID or Customer ID</li>
<li>Suspended Campaign ID or Ad ID</li>
<li>Screenshot of the violation notification email</li>
</ul>
<h3>Problem Explanation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clearly state which policy was violated (include policy name and link)</li>
<li>Explain the reason for the violation (misjudgment, creative issue, or landing page issue)</li>
<li>If it's a landing page issue, provide the corrected page URL</li>
</ul>
<h3>Corrective Evidence</h3>
<ul>
<li>Screenshot of removed or paused violating creatives</li>
<li>Evidence of landing page updates</li>
<li>Relevant qualification documents (business license, industry permits, etc., as applicable)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Commitment Statement</h3>
<ul>
<li>Brief description of corrective actions taken</li>
<li>Statement that you've reviewed the relevant policies and will strengthen review processes going forward</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>⚠️ Important:</strong> Materials must be authentic and specific. Generic statements like "We always comply with Google policies" won't help. The system values your understanding of the specific issue and the corrective actions you've taken.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. Which Appeal Channel to Use?</h2>
<p>A team asked this week: Is it faster to click "Request Review" directly in the dashboard or to email policy-support?</p>
<p><strong>The answer depends on your situation:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>If a campaign was paused, prioritize submitting through the "Request Review" channel in the Google Ads dashboard</li>
<li>If your account was suspended and the dashboard appeal is unavailable, try the official policy support form</li>
<li>For complex cases (such as trademark complaints or competitive disputes), consider CC'ing the official Google Ads Twitter/X account @GoogleAds</li>
</ul>
<p>This week also saw a change: <strong>some account responses switched from English to local languages</strong>, but the review logic remains unchanged. Language isn't the deciding factor—content accuracy is.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. This Week's Recommendations</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Build a Backup Account in Advance</strong>: If your business heavily relies on Google Ads, maintain a verified sub-account while your main account is stable. This allows quick switching if a sudden suspension occurs</li>
<li><strong>Conduct Weekly Account Self-Audits</strong>: Check landing page accessibility and creative compliance weekly. Proactive detection saves the hassle of reactive appeals</li>
<li><strong>Archive Your Appeal Materials</strong>: Keep copies of submitted materials for future reference when similar issues arise</li>
</ol>
<p>Account suspension isn't the end, but the appeal window is limited. If your account is under review or you're concerned about future risks, feel free to consult with our team. We can assist with account diagnostics and appeal material pre-review.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>This week, Yiyu Media continues monitoring overseas advertising platform policy changes. For real-time alerts, contact us with "Account Health" to subscribe.</em></p>
